module nupp.data

--[[
Bytes become values and values become bytes: `nupp.data` holds serialization,
Unicode, identifiers, byte digests and bitsets. Reach it from the global [`nupp`
namespace](docs/learn/runtime/data/standard-library.md), which is where the C and Rust
providers behind it stay hidden.

```nupp
local eventID = nupp.data.uuid7()
local digest = nupp.data.sha256("payload")
local visible = new nupp.data.Bitset(1024)
visible:set(37)
```

The small byte algorithms and identifiers live directly on `nupp.data`:
`fnv1a64`, `crc32`, `sha256`, `uuid4`, and `uuid7`. `Bitset` is its growable
set of bit positions, and `Key` and `Store` are its typed heterogeneous bag.
The facilities with their own substantial runtime remain modules of their own.

- See [](nupp.data.binary) for detached plain values with binary strings and
  numeric table keys.
- See [](nupp.data.json) for JSON encoding and decoding, and the values a Lua
  table cannot express by itself.
- See [](nupp.data.hash) for SHA-256 and HMAC-SHA256, over chunks or in one
  shot.
- See [](nupp.data.serde) for format-neutral schemas, typed bindings, dynamic
  values, and prepared codecs.
- See [](nupp.data.utf8) for codepoint operations over strings and byte views.
- See [](nupp.data.valuebuilder) for ordinary Lua values built straight out of
  parsed bytes.

The digests and the UTF-8 operations take a string or a
[](nupp.io.ByteView) interchangeably, so a parser holding a buffer
passes the view rather than converting it at every call site. JSON decoding
takes text.

## Choosing a digest

Three functions reduce bytes to a short value, and they promise different
things. Use `nupp.data.sha256` where the digest has to stand up to someone
choosing the input, `nupp.data.fnv1a64` for keying and bucketing, and
`nupp.data.crc32` for detecting accidental damage.

```nupp
local integrity = nupp.data.sha256(contents) -- 64 hex digits
local bucket = nupp.data.fnv1a64(key) -- 16 hex digits
```

They cost in the reverse order of what they promise, so reaching for the
strongest by default taxes every call that only needed a bucket.

## Bitsets

`new nupp.data.Bitset(bits)` creates a growable, zero-based bitset. Reading,
clearing, and testing beyond its current capacity are defined; setting beyond
it grows the private storage. Set algebra works a word at a time, and `count`
computes the population on demand.

## Keys and stores

A `Store` holds values of many types under identities that are not strings,
so a reader gets the type it stored and a writer cannot store the wrong one.
A `Key<T>` is that identity: an integer id and an optional name at runtime,
with `T` fixed by the annotation at its declaration and nowhere else.

```nupp
local record Settings
    volume: number
end

local settings: nupp.data.Key<Settings> = nupp.data.newKey("game.settings")
local frames: nupp.data.Key<integer> = nupp.data.newKey("game.frames")
local scratch: nupp.data.Key<string> = nupp.data.newKey(nil)

local world = nupp.data.newStore()
world[settings] = new Settings(volume = 0.5)
world[frames] = (world[frames] or 0) + 1
world[scratch] = "temporary"
world[scratch] = nil -- removes the entry

local current = world[settings] -- Settings?
assert(current ~= nil and current.volume == 0.5)
```

A read is `T?`, a write accepts `T?`, and `nil` removes the entry; `false`,
zero, and the empty string are stored as given. A wrong write through a key
is `NUPP2006`, a wrong read is `NUPP2001`, and a key does not widen or narrow
to another value type. A declaration with no annotation is `Key<any>`, which
the store checks nothing through, so the annotation is what makes a key typed.

Names are opaque strings registered once per runtime state. The empty name and
a name already registered raise before an id is used; anonymous keys are always
fresh. `findKey` returns the registered key as `unknown`, because the registry
knows the name's id and nothing about its type, and the caller casts to the
`Key<T>` it claims. `listKeys` returns a fresh table from name to id.

```nupp
local found = nupp.data.findKey("game.frames") as nupp.data.Key<integer>
assert(found.id == frames.id)
assert(nupp.data.listKeys()["game.frames"] == frames.id)
```

Ids are allocated in declaration order and identify a key inside this runtime
state only. A worker has its own registry, and a save file must never carry an
id: the name is the identity that persists, and
[schema-driven serde](docs/learn/runtime/data/serde.md#persisting-a-store) saves and
loads a store by it.

`clearStore` drops every value and touches no registration. `storeEntries`
walks the occupied keys ascending by id, reporting the id, the name, nil for an
anonymous key, and the Lua type name of the value, never the value itself. The
walk allocates nothing: the step is one shared function, the loop state is the
store, and ids are dense and ascending, so the order is the sorted order
without a sort. `nextStoreEntry` is that step on its own, for a caller holding
the store as a borrow, which cannot hand it to a loop.

```nupp
for id, name, valueType in nupp.data.storeEntries(world) do
    assert(id > 0 and type(valueType) == "string")
end
```

[NEP 34](docs/neps/0034-typed-keys-and-stores.md) records why a key is shaped
this way, and why serde's `MetadataKey` and reflection's `ExtensionKey` are
the same key.

## Native selection

Which functions are native is not part of the contract. The build selects the
UUID provider only when generated code reaches that exact member. See
[Compiler-native features](docs/learn/projects/build.md#compiler-native-features) for
how feature selection works and how to override it.

SHA-256 used to be one of them and is not any more. It is
[](nupp.data.digest): Nupp, compiled ahead of time where the build asks for
that, and the same digest on every target either way.
]]

local data = {}
local bitset = require("nupp.data.internal.bitset")
local store = require("nupp.data.internal.store")
const digest = require("nupp.data.digest")
const native = require("nupp.runtime.native")

native.ffi.cdef[[
int32_t nuppNativeV2Uuid4(uint8_t *, size_t);
int32_t nuppNativeV2Uuid7(uint8_t *, size_t);
]]

const C = native.C

--- How many bits one stored word holds.
--- @export
const data.WORD_BITS = bitset.WORD_BITS

local CRC_TABLE = {}
for entry = 0, 255 do
    local current = entry
    for _ = 1, 8 do
        current = bit.bxor(bit.rshift(current, 1), bit.band(current, 1) ~= 0 and 0xedb88320 or 0)
    end
    CRC_TABLE[entry] = current
end

--- Hashes bytes with FNV-1a over 64 bits.
---
--- @param value the bytes to hash
--- @return the hash, as sixteen lowercase hexadecimal digits
--- @export
function data.fnv1a64(value: string | nupp.io.ByteView): string
    local bytes = type(value) == "string" and value as string or (value as nupp.io.ByteView):getString()
    local hi = 0xcbf29ce4
    local lo = 0x84222325
    for index = 1, #bytes do
        lo = bit.bxor(lo, bytes:byte(index))
        local low = lo < 0 and lo + 4294967296 or lo
        local high = hi < 0 and hi + 4294967296 or hi
        local product = low * 435
        local carry = math.floor(product / 4294967296)
        lo = bit.tobit(product)
        hi = bit.tobit(high * 435 + low * 256 + carry)
    end
    local high = hi < 0 and hi + 4294967296 or hi
    local low = lo < 0 and lo + 4294967296 or lo

    return string.format("%08x%08x", high, low)
end

--- Checksums bytes with CRC-32.
---
--- Passing a previous checksum continues one checksum across several buffers.
--- @param value the bytes to checksum
--- @param previous a checksum to continue from, or nil to start one
--- @return the checksum, as an unsigned 32-bit integer
--- @raises when previous is not an unsigned 32-bit integer
--- @export
function data.crc32(value: string | nupp.io.ByteView, previous: integer?): integer
    local carried = previous
    if carried == nil then
        carried = 0
    elseif carried < 0 or carried > 4294967295 then
        error("nupp: crc32 previous checksum must be an unsigned 32-bit integer", 2)
    end
    local current = bit.bnot(carried)
    local bytes = type(value) == "string" and value as string or (value as nupp.io.ByteView):getString()
    for index = 1, #bytes do
        current = bit.bxor(bit.rshift(current, 8), CRC_TABLE[bit.band(bit.bxor(current, bytes:byte(index)), 255)])
    end
    local answer = bit.bnot(current)

    return answer < 0 and answer + 4294967296 or answer
end

--- Hashes bytes with SHA-256.
---
--- @param value the bytes to hash
--- @return the digest, as sixty-four lowercase hexadecimal digits
--- @raises when the input cannot be hashed
--- @export
function data.sha256(value: string | nupp.io.ByteView): string
    local bytes = type(value) == "string" and value as string or (value as nupp.io.ByteView):getString()

    return digest.sha256(bytes)
end

--- Generates a random version 4 UUID.
---
--- @return the UUID, hyphenated and lowercase
--- @raises when the system has no randomness to draw on
--- @export
function data.uuid4(): string
    native.requireFeature(2, "UUID support")
    local output = native.ffi.new("uint8_t[37]")
    native.succeeded(C.nuppNativeV2Uuid4(output, 37), 2)

    return native.ffi.string(output)
end

--- Generates a time-ordered version 7 UUID.
---
--- @return the UUID, hyphenated and lowercase
--- @raises when the system has no randomness to draw on
--- @export
function data.uuid7(): string
    native.requireFeature(2, "UUID support")
    local output = native.ffi.new("uint8_t[37]")
    native.succeeded(C.nuppNativeV2Uuid7(output, 37), 2)

    return native.ffi.string(output)
end

--- A growable zero-based set of bit positions.
export type Bitset = bitset.Bitset

data.Bitset = bitset.Bitset

--- A typed identity for one value in a [](nupp.data.Store).
export type Key<T> = store.Key<T>

--- An independent typed bag indexed by keys.
export type Store = store.Store

--- The step of [](nupp.data.storeEntries).
export type StoreStep = store.StoreStep

--- Creates a key whose type argument comes from the annotation at its declaration.
---
--- @param name the opaque name to register, or nil for an anonymous key
--- @return the key
--- @raises when the name is empty or already registered
--- @export
function data.newKey<T>(name: string?): Key<T>
    return store.newKey(name)
end

--- Finds a registered key by name; the caller casts the `unknown` result.
---
--- @param name the registered name
--- @return the key, or nil when the name is not registered
--- @export
function data.findKey(name: string): unknown
    return store.findKey(name)
end

--- Returns a fresh table from every registered name to its id.
--- @return the caller-owned listing
--- @export
function data.listKeys(): {[string]: integer}
    return store.listKeys()
end

--- Creates an empty store.
--- @return the store
--- @export
function data.newStore(): Store
    return store.new()
end

--- Removes every value from a store without touching any registration.
--- @param value the store to clear
--- @return nothing
--- @export
function data.clearStore(value: Store): nil
    store.clear(value)
end

--- Walks every occupied key of a store, ascending by id, without its values.
--- @param value the store to walk
--- @return the step the loop calls
--- @return the store it walks
--- @return the id to resume after, 0 to start
--- @export
function data.storeEntries(value: Store): (StoreStep, Store, integer)
    return store.entries(value)
end

--- Reports the first occupied key after an id, or nil when there is none.
---
--- This is the step [](nupp.data.storeEntries) hands the generic for. A caller
--- holding the store as a borrow cannot pass it to `storeEntries`, so it names
--- the step and the state itself:
---
--- ```nupp
--- for id, name in nupp.data.nextStoreEntry as nupp.data.StoreStep, store, 0 do
--- ```
--- @param value the store to read
--- @param after the id to resume after, 0 to start
--- @return the id, or nil once the walk is done
--- @return the key's name, nil for an anonymous key
--- @return the Lua type name of the value
--- @export
function data.nextStoreEntry(borrows value: Store, after: integer): (integer?, string?, string?)
    return store.nextEntry(value, after)
end

export = data
